Shigellosis
Infectious Disease & Sexual Health
Frequent small-volume bloody/mucus stools, Agonizing tenesmus, Severe lower abdominal cramping, High fever/systemic toxicity

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: IDS-049
Reason for Visit: Acute, agonizing lower abdominal cramps, high fever, and frequent bloody stools requiring urgent assessment to rule out an acute surgical abdomen or severe toxic colitis.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated a patient experiencing extreme systemic toxicity, violent rigors, and agonizing, relentless urges to defecate passing only small amounts of blood and mucus. Urgent physical assessment is required to evaluate for acute peritonitis, assess the risk of toxic megacolon, and aggressively manage dehydration prior to the next available in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "I'm in agonizing pain, I constantly feel like I need to go to the toilet, but all that comes out is blood and slime."
History of Present Illness: Illness commenced acutely 24 hours ago with high fever, severe distinct shaking chills, and extreme generalized systemic toxicity. The patient developed severe, continuous, and agonizing lower abdominal cramping. This was rapidly followed by the sudden onset of frequent (20+ times a day), distinctly small-volume stools containing prominent macroscopic blood, thick mucus, and distinct pus. The patient describes profound, intense tenesmus—an agonizing, distinct painful urge to continuously defecate. The patient reports nausea, recurrent severe vomiting, and profound absolute anorexia. Recently returned from distinct travel to an endemic region (developing nation) 5 days ago.
Functional Status: Bedbound, highly toxic, running to the toilet every 15-30 minutes.
Social/Safety Concerns: Lives alone; the profound toxicity and continuous pain are causing severe acute distress.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 100/65 | HR: 120 | Temp: 39.8 | RR: 20 | SpO2: 98% | Wt: 70 Kg
General: Acutely toxic, diaphoretic, flushed, visibly writhing during bouts of abdominal cramping.
Physical Exam: Abdominal exam reveals hyperactive bowel sounds. There is severe, diffuse lower quadrant tenderness to deep palpation, but no board-like rigidity or distinct rebound tenderness. Digital rectal exam is deferred due to severe tenesmus and pain, but inspection of the toilet reveals a small volume of frank blood and thick purulent mucus. Hydration assessment reveals dry mucous membranes, sunken eyes, and a delayed capillary refill (3-4 seconds). Clinical presentation aligns with the diagnostic tetrad: Frequent small-volume bloody/mucus stools, Agonizing tenesmus, Severe lower abdominal cramping, and High fever/systemic toxicity.
Environmental Observations: Patient is exhausted from the constant trips to the bathroom.
Medication Reconciliation: No regular medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Shigellosis (Acute Bacillary Dysentery) - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Inflammatory Bowel Disease (Severe Ulcerative Colitis flare) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The acute, sudden onset strictly linked to endemic travel, the massive fever/rigors, and the complete lack of prior chronic bowel symptoms.

Acute Appendicitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The presence of frank bloody/mucus stools and profound tenesmus, which indicate severe colonic inflammation (colitis) rather than isolated right lower quadrant appendiceal pathology.

Intussusception - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Patient age (adult), the presence of high fever and systemic toxicity, though the "red currant jelly" stool can look identical.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with classic, severe bacillary dysentery (Shigellosis), characterized by small-volume bloody stools, extreme tenesmus, and profound systemic toxicity. The agonizing abdominal pain and high fever necessitated an immediate, in-person clinical examination to confidently rule out a perforated bowel, assess for impending toxic megacolon, and evaluate the degree of intravascular volume depletion. Given the severity of the symptoms and the risk of rapid clinical deterioration or severe localized complications, delaying assessment and the initiation of targeted antibiotic therapy (which is indicated for Shigella to shorten the severe illness duration and reduce transmission) until the next in-hours period was clinically unsafe, satisfying the MBS 585-600 criteria.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Advise absolute contraindication of all anti-motility agents (e.g., Loperamide), as these can precipitate toxic megacolon in Shigellosis. Prescribe empiric targeted oral antibiotics (e.g., Ciprofloxacin or Azithromycin, pending local resistance patterns) to rapidly reduce the bacterial load and toxicity. Prescribe aggressive oral rehydration solution (ORS). Arrange for an urgent stool multiplex PCR and culture tomorrow.
Positive Behavioral Change: By taking the prescribed antibiotic exactly 30 minutes after a small amount of bland food, the patient will minimize gastric irritation; successfully retaining the antibiotic will result in a rapid, noticeable decrease in the agonizing tenesmus within 24 hours, powerfully reinforcing adherence to the full antibiotic course.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Mandatory notification to the State Public Health Unit.
DME/Safety (Optional): Bedside commode if mobility becomes unsafe.
Follow-up: Urgent GP review in 24-48 hours to review stool culture results and monitor for resolution of bleeding.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ED presentation or call 000 if the patient develops a completely rigid, board-like abdomen, stops passing urine for more than 8 hours, becomes profoundly confused, or if the bleeding becomes massive and continuous.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is presumptive bacillary dysentery; specific stool cultures are required to confirm the bacteria and its antibiotic susceptibilities.
Clinical Course: The extreme toxicity and agonizing cramps should begin to significantly improve within 48 hours of starting the targeted antibiotics.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

The bacteria have caused severe, bloody ulcers in your lower bowel. You must go to the emergency department immediately if your stomach becomes completely hard and rigid like a board, as this could mean the infection has worn a hole through the bowel wall.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: You have contracted a highly aggressive bacterial infection that specifically attacks the lining of your large intestine. Unlike normal food poisoning, this bacteria requires strong antibiotics to kill it and stop the bloody diarrhoea. You must never take anti-diarrhoea medication for this, as it will trap the dangerous bacteria inside your bowel and can cause your colon to rupture.
Actionable Steps: Start the antibiotics immediately. Drink oral rehydration fluids continuously. Wash your hands meticulously with soap and water; Shigella is extremely contagious even in microscopic amounts.

